# Line endings.
#
# This matters more here than in most repositories, because line endings are
# not incidental to this tool: they are part of what it is measured on. The
# application detects the ending a script was written with, carries it on the
# graph, and puts it back when it writes the file, because opening a CRLF
# script and saving it must not change every line in the file. 3,776 of the
# 4,765 files in the vanilla and installed-mod corpus are CRLF. importtest
# holds that down with two checks that have no room under them: every class
# comes back byte for byte, and endingLost == 0.
#
# Git for Windows sets core.autocrlf=true in its system config, in
# C:/Program Files/Git/etc/gitconfig, so every Windows contributor has it
# switched on whether they chose it or not. Left alone, a fresh clone would
# rewrite every text file in the working tree to CRLF, including the fixtures
# the byte-comparison tests read. The rules below take that decision away from
# whatever each machine happens to be configured with.

# Normalise to LF in the repository and check out LF everywhere. The index is
# already LF for all 261 tracked text files, so this changes no stored bytes;
# it stops the working tree drifting to CRLF on Windows.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Everything under resources/ is frozen. These are not sources, they are test
# inputs, and several tests compare their bytes:
#
#   templatetest    copies the mod template and proves the copy is exact
#   configtest      parses config.cpp and writes it back byte for byte
#   coretest        round trips SUDO_Link.sdzn with every node and edge intact
#   importtest      generates .c files and compares them to the originals
#
# The bundled mod template is also third party content under its own MIT
# licence, and the right thing to do with somebody else's files is to keep the
# bytes they shipped. tools/build-catalog.mjs reproduces catalog.json byte for
# byte, which stops being a checkable claim if checkout can rewrite it.
#
# -text, not binary: these files should still produce readable diffs.
resources/** -text

# A .sdzn is a file the application itself writes, and its round trip is
# compared byte for byte. Freeze them wherever they live, not only under
# resources/, so an example project committed alongside documentation is held
# to the same rule as a fixture.
*.sdzn -text

# Genuinely binary. `binary` is shorthand for `-text -diff`.
*.png binary
*.ico binary
*.jpg binary
*.paa binary
*.pbo binary
*.pyc binary

# GitHub language statistics and diff collapsing. catalog.json is 2.9 MB of
# generated output from tools/build-catalog.mjs, and the mod template is
# vendored from https://github.com/InclementDab/DayZ-Mod-Template. Neither is
# code written for this project, and neither is useful to read in a diff.
resources/catalog.json linguist-generated=true
resources/mod-template/** linguist-vendored=true
